@title the four ways of saying something out loud

You always speak in the language you have selected, and anybody who
does not know it hears your words garbled. Use 'speak' to see which
ones you know and to change from one to another.

  say <text>

Everybody where you are hears it. The closing punctuation changes
the verb: "say hello" comes out as 'You say: hello', "say anybody
there?" as 'You ask: ...' and "say hey!" as 'You exclaim: ...'.

  whisper [to] <person> <text>

Only that person hears you, and the rest see that you whispered
something to them. They have to be where you are. The word 'to' is
optional.

  tell <person> <message>

Reaches them wherever they are, at the other end of the world if
need be. It needs a language that can be spoken at a distance, it
spends social points, and the other person has to be connected. A
nickname works in place of the name.

  shout <text>

Everybody hears it. It costs social points, more the longer the
shout is, and afterwards you need a couple of seconds to catch your
breath before shouting again. It also needs a language that can be
spoken at a distance, and you cannot shout with your earmuffs on.
Shouts are logged.
